WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:10.560
Each person's genome has about three million&nbsp;
single nucleotide variations, as well as many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:16.080
other types of genetic variations. How do we&nbsp;
predict the function of these genetic variations?&nbsp;

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:24.720
On May 14th 2013, movie star Angelina Jolie&nbsp;
revealed that she has a mutation in her BRCA1 gene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:30.640
and that her mother died early from breast cancer.&nbsp;
To reduce her own risk of getting breast cancer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:34.400
she made the drastic decision to have&nbsp;
both of her breasts surgically removed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:38.240
This revelation sent shock&nbsp;
waves all over the world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:42.400
Many people wondered and argued&nbsp;
whether she had made the right choice.&nbsp;

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:48.400
The core of Angelina Jolie's decision touches&nbsp;
upon an important bioinformatics question.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:54.960
Given that she has a genetic mutation in BRCA1,&nbsp;
what is the conditional probability that she will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.960 --> 00:01:00.560
develop breast cancer? Even with her mutation,&nbsp;
there is a chance that she may be cancer free.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:06.880
These two probabilities add up to one.
Before we talk about how to calculate these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:12.640
numbers, let's first look at where our genetic&nbsp;
variations come from. We all know that we inherit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:19.440
genetic variations from our parents. If our mother&nbsp;
carries a particular mutation allele, there is 50%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:25.440
chance that we may inherit it from her. Each of&nbsp;
us also have over 70 de novo single-nucleotide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:31.200
mutations that neither of our parents have.&nbsp;
Many severe childhood neurological disorders&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:35.920
such as Rett syndrome are caused by de novo&nbsp;
mutations that disrupted critical genes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:42.640
Each of us also has somatic mutations, that&nbsp;
are accumulated during cell divisions. An&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:47.840
extreme example of this is cancer, where somatic&nbsp;
mutations cause cells to grow uncontrollably.&nbsp;

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:54.400
There are many types of genetic variations in&nbsp;
the human genome. Different genetic variations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:57.680
may have very different effects on&nbsp;
the genome and on the phenotype.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:03.360
The most severe is chromosomal aneuploidy,&nbsp;
meaning that a person has one or more copies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:09.280
of actual chromosomes or missing chromosomes.&nbsp;
The best known example is Down Syndrome, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:15.520
is caused by three copies of chromosome 21 or&nbsp;
Trisomy 21 which almost always have been de novo.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:21.040
We never hear about Trisomy 1 or Trisomy&nbsp;
2 in humans, because these chromosomes are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:26.000
so large and contain so many genes that having&nbsp;
abnormal copies of them is embryonically lethal.&nbsp;

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:32.080
Another type of microscopic or submicroscopic&nbsp;
genetic variation in the human genome is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:39.760
structural variations. They include deletions,&nbsp;
duplications, inversion, insertions and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:45.360
translocation. Deletion is a type of mutation&nbsp;
involving the loss of genetic material.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:52.320
Insertion is a type of mutation involving the&nbsp;
addition of genetic material. Many mobile elements&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:58.320
are inserted in each of our genomes. A novel&nbsp;
sequence, such as virus genomes are sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:04.960
inserted into our genome. There are 2 types&nbsp;
of genetic duplications. Tandem duplications&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:09.520
happen where a segment of a chromosome is&nbsp;
duplicated right next to the original copy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:15.360
Interspersed duplications happen where a segment&nbsp;
of the chromosome is duplicated to somewhere else&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:21.680
on the genome. Deletions and duplications are&nbsp;
often called copy number variations or CNVs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:27.600
Finally, two other types of genetic structural&nbsp;
variation that cause disruptions of the genome&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:31.840
but not genomic balance include inversions&nbsp;
where a segment of a chromosome is inverted.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:37.440
And translocations where a segment of a chromosome&nbsp;
is moved to somewhere else on the genome.&nbsp;

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:44.000
Short insertions and deletions of one or a few,&nbsp;
up to a thousand or several thousands nucleotides,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:49.360
sometimes called indels. Indels may&nbsp;
happen in intergenic or intronic regions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:54.720
or they may happen within protein coding&nbsp;
regions. Within protein coding regions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:58.720
if an indel involves three or&nbsp;
multiplicity of three nucleotides,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:04.960
it will only add or delete one or several codons&nbsp;
without causing a frameshift. Otherwise, it may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:10.640
cause a frameshift that may result in a complete&nbsp;
changed of the amino acid sequence. Often it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:16.800
would also cause a premature stop codon. The new&nbsp;
protein may not be stable enough to exist at all.&nbsp;

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:22.880
At a smaller scale, but with much higher&nbsp;
frequency, there are single nucleotide variations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:30.800
On average, in a person's genome, there is about 3&nbsp;
million SNVs, roughly equivalent to 1 SNV in every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:37.360
1,000 nucleotides. SNVs that are known to affect&nbsp;
functions and phenotypes the most are located in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:44.080
the promoter regions or protein coding regions&nbsp;
of genes. Variations in UTR and intronic regions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:50.240
sometimes also affect function. Most variants&nbsp;
are located in intergenic regions between genes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:55.840
Some of them fall in non-coding RNA&nbsp;
that are transcribed but not translated.&nbsp;

00:04:56.640 --> 00:05:01.920
SNVs within coding regions tend to have larger&nbsp;
effects than other variations and those have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:08.480
studied the most. In the more severe cases, SNV&nbsp;
can cause a premature stop codon that terminates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.480 --> 00:05:14.560
a protein early. In this example shown, the&nbsp;
cytosine nucleotide is changed to thymine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:20.640
As a result, the codon CUG that used&nbsp;
to encode amino acid of glutamine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:26.000
now it becomes TAG which is a stop codon&nbsp;
resulting in premature termination of the protein.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:33.920
This SNV is called a nonsense mutation. Because&nbsp;
a nonsense SNV causes premature termination of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:39.200
protein, it is usually predicted to be damaging,&nbsp;
even though there are exceptions where paralogous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:43.200
proteins or alternative pathways can&nbsp;
compensate for the loss of a protein.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:48.160
In the second example, the adenine&nbsp;
nucleotide is changed to cytosine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.120 --> 00:05:55.520
As a result, the codon CAT that used to encode&nbsp;
the amino acid histidine now it becomes CCT&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.520 --> 00:06:02.720
which encodes proline. This SNV is called a&nbsp;
non-synonymous or missense SNV. Because the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.720 --> 00:06:09.440
codons are degenerative, many SNVs do not cause&nbsp;
amino acid changes. They are called synonymous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.440 --> 00:06:16.320
or same sense or silent SNVs. Some SNVs at or&nbsp;
near splice injunctions may affect splicing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:23.120
And finally, some SNVs change a stop codon to&nbsp;
a codon encoding amino acid, resulting in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:27.600
lessening of the protein which may have altered&nbsp;
or disrupted stability, structure and function.&nbsp;

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:35.520
Synonymous, intronic, and intergenic variations&nbsp;
are often ignored. However, according to GWAS&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:42.480
studies, 88% of trait-associated variations of&nbsp;
weak effect are non-coding. Although, individually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:47.440
their functional effects may not be as obvious,&nbsp;
because these regions are so large, their total&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:53.760
effects cannot be neglected, especially, a mild&nbsp;
traits. However, they remain under-studied,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:59.680
and better methods are still needed.
Most research so far had focused on missense SNVs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.640 --> 00:07:05.360
A big reason is because known deleterious&nbsp;
mutations are enriched in missense mutations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.320 --> 00:07:11.280
About 50% of all known mutations of&nbsp;
Mendelian disorders are missense mutations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:16.000
However, it is important to note that&nbsp;
there might be ascertainment biases&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:20.080
because an important discovery tends to&nbsp;
attract more research in the same direction.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.040 --> 00:07:24.640
However, not all missense&nbsp;
SNVs cause phenotypic changes.&nbsp;

00:07:25.760 --> 00:07:30.160
For instance, BRCA1 was the first&nbsp;
gene associated with breast cancer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.160 --> 00:07:35.760
in 1990 based on linkage analysis of large&nbsp;
pedigrees of early onset familial breast cancer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:43.840
BRCA1 has a total of 238 known missense&nbsp;
mutations, 163 are present only in patients,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:49.520
62 are present only in healthy persons, and&nbsp;
13 in both patients and healthy persons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.480 --> 00:07:54.960
Furthermore, even missense variations&nbsp;
seen only in patients are not all causal.&nbsp;

00:07:56.000 --> 00:08:01.520
If we look more broadly, analysis of the whole&nbsp;
genomes of over 1,000 healthy individuals in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.520 --> 00:08:08.080
1,000 Genome Project revealed that, on average,&nbsp;
a healthy individual carries over 3 million SNPs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:15.600
over 361,000 indels, almost 16,000&nbsp;
deletions, over 400 duplications,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:21.920
and almost 5,000 mobile element insertions.&nbsp;
Within protein-coding regions, on average,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.920 --> 00:08:28.080
a healthy individual carries large division&nbsp;
that disrupt about 150 genes over 1,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.080 --> 00:08:37.120
stop coding SNPs, 77 stop losses, over 900 small&nbsp;
frameshift indels, over 700 small in-frame indels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.120 --> 00:08:44.560
nearly 70,000 non-synonymous SNPs and 60,000&nbsp;
synonymous SNPs. So, the questions are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.560 --> 00:08:50.160
what features differentiate disease-causing&nbsp;
variants from neutral ones? How can we predict&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:56.400
whether a variation is disease-causing? Unlike&nbsp;
sequence alignment and sequence database search,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.400 --> 00:09:07.840
the questions here remain largely unsolved. And&nbsp;
there's still lots of active researches going on.

